class:: PolyaUrn
summary:: Triggered choices that reinforce their own future selection probability
categories:: UGens>Generators>Stochastic>State
related:: Classes/MarkovChain, Classes/SemiMarkov, Classes/TDirichletRand, Guides/Stochastica

description::
A Pólya urn stores accumulated choice weights. At each trigger it selects state i with probability code::w[i]/sum(w)::, then adds reinforcement to that state's weight. Early chance selections can become persistent preferences. Unlike MarkovChain, which remembers the current state and selects a transition row, the urn remembers the entire history through its weights. Unlike TDirichletRand, it does not redraw an independent allocation each time. Zero reinforcement gives fixed categorical sampling.

The selectedProbability output reports the chance of the selected state before reinforcement. normalizedEntropy describes all probabilities after reinforcement: code::-sum(p*log(p))/log(numStates)::. One means equal diversity; zero means one surviving category. Entropy need not decrease monotonically, and a balanced ensemble of urns can contain very different individual preferences. Source: the classical reinforced urn described in Pemantle's link::https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0610076:: survey.

The initial Buffer is a snapshot and is never rewritten. Private weights are proportionally rescaled above total 2^40; future reinforcement is converted into the same units, preserving the additive model apart from rounding. Each draw is O(N), including entropy, with N at most 64. Extremely small probabilities can eventually be below floating precision; this is not an explicit elimination rule.

Vanilla link::Classes/TWindex:: also selects a weighted category. PolyaUrn carries the reinforcing weight history inside the UGen and exposes its diversity.

image::round3-urn.png::

subsection:: See also
link::Classes/MarkovChain::, link::Classes/SemiMarkov::, link::Classes/TDirichletRand::, link::Guides/Stochastica::

classmethods::
method:: ar, kr
argument:: numStates
Construction-time state count, clipped to 2..64.
argument:: weightBuf
Mono Buffer with at least numStates finite nonnegative base weights and positive total. Invalid data falls back to equal weights. Zero-weight states cannot be selected. Copied at construction/reload; user data is never modified.
argument:: trig
Nonpositive-to-positive selection edge. Construction selects no random state; held default trig=1 needs a fresh edge. Outputs hold between events.
argument:: reinforcement
Nonnegative amount added after a selection, sampled on that edge. Negative/non-finite values become zero.
argument:: valueBuf
Optional mono Buffer mapping states to finite values. Negative, missing, short or non-finite buffers fall back to indices. Copied with weightBuf.
argument:: reload
Rising edge replaces base weights and values, restores the current urn to those weights and state zero, and recomputes diagnostics without drawing or rewinding RNG. Suppresses a coincident selection.
argument:: seed
Local PCG32 seed. Nonzero repeats a sequence; zero derives from the parent Synth only at construction.
argument:: reset
Rising edge restores cached base weights, state zero and the original local seed. Has priority over reload and selection without rereading the Buffer. Simultaneous edges are consumed.
returns::
Four outputs: code::[stateIndex, stateValue, selectedProbability, normalizedEntropy]::. State indices are zero-based. At construction/reset/reload, probability is state zero's base probability and entropy is the base distribution's entropy. Allocation failure produces silence.

examples::
subsection:: Minimal reinforced choice
code::
(
Routine {
    var weights = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [1, 1]);
    var node;
    s.sync;
    node = { SinOsc.ar(220 * (PolyaUrn.kr(2, weights, Impulse.kr(6), seed: 17)[0] + 1), 0, 0.07) ! 2 }.play;
    12.wait; node.free; s.sync; weights.free;
}.play;
)
::

subsection:: Preference and audible diversity
Four registers compete. Falling entropy narrows the stereo field, exposing the diversity of the current preference as well as the selected category. A fixed Markov transition matrix would condition each choice on the previous category; this example's probabilities reflect every prior choice.
code::
(
Routine {
    var weights = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [1, 1, 1, 1]);
    var values = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [165, 330, 660, 1320]);
    var node;
    s.sync;
    node = {
        var trigger = Impulse.kr(8);
        var urn = PolyaUrn.kr(4, weights, trigger, 1, values, seed: 48);
        var position = urn[0].linlin(0, 3, -1, 1) * urn[3];
        Pan2.ar(SinOsc.ar(urn[1], 0, Decay2.kr(trigger, 0.003, 0.09) * 0.09), position)
            * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.05)
    }.play;
    25.wait; node.free; s.sync; [weights, values].do(_.free);
}.play;
)
::
